tel 1.0

base caesura
import soundness

info   CSV, TSV and other delimited text: rows parsed under a chosen format, and decoded to and from case classes
detail
    _Caesura_ reads and writes delimiter-separated values — CSV, its tab-separated cousin TSV,
    and space-separated data — with the quoting, escaping and header conventions decided by a
    `Dsv.Format` chosen in scope rather than guessed. Text parses into a `Sheet`, a
    materialized array of rows; each row is a `Dsv`, an array of cells which knows its column
    names when the data had a header; and a row decodes into a case class, or a case class
    encodes into a row, through a derivation from the type, so the mapping from columns to
    fields is never written by hand.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import dsvFormats.csvFormat
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class FullName(first: Text, last: Text)
    case class Person(id: Int, name: FullName, age: Int)

    val sheet: Sheet = t"1,Nick,Bottom,36\n2,Francis,Flute,41".read[Sheet]
    val nick: Person = t"1,Nick,Bottom,36".read[Person in Dsv]
    val people: List[Person] = sheet.as[Person]
    val text: Text = people.dsv.show   // t"1,Nick,Bottom,36\n2,Francis,Flute,41"
    ```

    A nested case class flattens across as many columns as it has fields (its _span_, the
    `Spannable` typeclass), an `Optional` field reads a short row as `Unset`, and with a
    header-bearing format each field is matched to the column of its own name, so the order
    of the columns need not match the order of the fields.

    There are two ways from text to a value. The **row decoder** builds a `Dsv` for each row
    and decodes it with a `Decodable in Dsv`, which every product type derives; under an
    accruing strategy it reports every bad cell, each focused on a `CellRef`. The **direct
    parser** reads a type which has a `Dsv.Parsable` straight off the parser's reusable row
    buffer, building no `Dsv` at all, which is the fast path for large files. The `codecs`
    modes (`codecs.direct`, `codecs.derived`, `codecs.strict`) choose between them as they do
    for every other format. A file too large to hold is read with `rows` or `rowsOf` on a
    character stream, which yields one row at a time.

    The choices are givens in packages, each imported by name, because `import soundness.*`
    does not import givens:

    - `dsvFormats` — the format: `csvFormat`, `tsvFormat` and `ssvFormat`, each with a
      `…WithHeaderFormat` variant. Nothing parses or renders without one.
    - `dsvRedesignations` — how a camel-case member name is mapped to a header name for
      dynamic access, such as `capitalizedWordsRedesignation` (`targetPerson` to
      `Target Person`).
    - `dynamicAccess` — `dynamicDsv`, which lets a row's cells be read as members,
      `row.age[Int]`.
    - `optionalityOptions` — whether an `Optional` field treats a missing or a malformed cell
      as `Unset` or as an error.
    - `optics` — lenses over a row's cells and a sheet's rows, for `panopticon`'s `lens`.
    - `construables` — the media types `text/csv` and `text/tab-separated-values` construed
      as a `Sheet`, for HTTP clients.

    The library has two components. `caesura.core` has everything but the media-type
    mappings, and is platform-neutral. `caesura.http` adds the `construables` package, and
    depends on `gesticulate`. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the
    choice packages merge with the umbrella's own packages of the same names.

refer caesura.Dsv
refer caesura.Sheet
refer caesura.Dsv.Format
refer caesura.dsvFormats
refer caesura.Dsv.Parsable
refer caesura.Spannable

entry .Dsv   one row of delimited data: an array of cells, with the column names where the data had a header
  detail
      A `Dsv` is a single row: its `#data` is the row's cells as `Text`, in order, and its
      `#columns` maps each column name to its index when the row was parsed under a
      header-bearing format (and is `Unset` otherwise). It is the value a `Sheet`'s rows are
      made of, what `rows` yields from a stream, and the `Form` of every row codec: a type
      is `Decodable in Dsv` if it can be read from a row, and `Encodable in Dsv` if it can be
      written as one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvWithHeaderFormat
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Measurement(name: Text, age: Int)

      val row: Optional[Dsv] = t"name,age\nPercy,30".read[Sheet].rows(Prim)
      row.let(_.as[Measurement])          // Measurement(t"Percy", 30)
      row.let(_[Int](t"age"))             // 30
      Dsv(t"a", t"b, c").show             // t"a,\"b, c\""
      ```

      The companion holds every codec, so none needs an import: cell decoders for `Int`,
      `Long`, `Double`, `Float`, `Boolean`, `Text` and `String`; a fallback which reads the
      first cell of a row as any type `Decodable in Text` (in `Dsv2`); `Optional` fields;
      encoding of any type `Encodable in Text` as a one-cell row; the derivations of row
      decoders and encoders for every product type; and the `Aggregable` givens behind
      `read[Play in Dsv]` and `read[List[Play] in Dsv]` (some in its parents, `Dsv1` and
      `Dsv2`). It also has `Format`, `Error`, `Redesignation`, and the direct-parsing
      typeclasses `Parsable` and `Field`.

      The primitive cell decoders do not abort on a bad cell: they raise a `Dsv.Error` and
      continue with a placeholder, so that when a row decodes into a product under an
      accruing strategy (`Validate[…, CellRef]`) every malformed or missing cell is reported,
      each focused on the `CellRef` of its column, rather than only the first.

      With `dynamicAccess.dynamicDsv` imported and a `Dsv.Redesignation` in scope, a cell is a
      member of the row: `row.age[Int]` (see `#selectDynamic`).
  refer caesura.Sheet
  refer caesura.Dsv.Format
  refer caesura.Dsv.Parsable
  refer caesura.CellRef
  entry .apply   makes a row from its cells, given as arguments or as an `Iterable[Text]`
      `Dsv(t"a", t"b")` and `Dsv(cells)` make a row with no column names. A row with column
      names comes from parsing under a header-bearing format, or from the case-class
      constructor, `Dsv(data, columns)`.
  entry .optionalDecodable   decodes an `Optional` field: a missing cell is `Unset`, a present one is decoded by the inner type
      A row with no cells — a short positional row, or a header column the row lacks — decodes
      to `Unset`; a present cell, even an empty one, is decoded by the inner type's
      `Decodable in Dsv`. Both answers are policies chosen in `optionalityOptions`: a strict
      `Decodable.Absence in Dsv` raises `Dsv.Error.Reason.Absent` for a missing cell, and a
      lenient `Decodable.Fault in Dsv` reads a cell the inner decoder rejects as `Unset`.
      The defaults are lenient absence and strict faults. Needs a `Dsv.Format` and a
      `Tactic[Dsv.Error]`.
  entry .encoder   writes any type which is `Encodable in Text` as a one-cell row
  entry .int   decodes the first cell of a row as an `Int`, raising `Dsv.Error` and continuing if it cannot
      As every primitive cell decoder: a missing cell raises `Dsv.Error.Reason.Absent` and an
      unparseable one `Dsv.Error.Reason.Unparseable(cell, t"Int")`, each through `raise`, so
      that an accruing strategy collects the error and decoding goes on with `0`. Needs a
      `Dsv.Format` and a `Tactic[Dsv.Error]`.
  entry .long   decodes the first cell of a row as a `Long`, raising `Dsv.Error` and continuing if it cannot
  entry .double   decodes the first cell of a row as a `Double`, raising `Dsv.Error` and continuing if it cannot
  entry .float   decodes the first cell of a row as a `Float`, raising `Dsv.Error` and continuing if it cannot
  entry .boolean   decodes the first cell of a row as a `Boolean`: only `true` and `false` are accepted
      Any other text, including `TRUE` or `1`, raises `Dsv.Error.Reason.Unparseable` and
      decoding continues with `false`.
  entry .text   decodes the first cell of a row as `Text`; raises `Dsv.Error` only if the cell is missing
  entry .string   decodes the first cell of a row as a `String`; raises `Dsv.Error` only if the cell is missing
  entry .decodableDerivation   derives a row decoder for any product type, taking its fields' cells in turn
      Each field takes as many cells as its span (`Spannable`): one for a field which is
      `Decodable in Text`, and the sum of its own fields' spans for a nested case class. Under
      a header-bearing format, each field is instead found by the column of its own name,
      exactly as the field is spelt, and a field whose column is absent receives an empty
      row, so an `Optional` field is `Unset`. Under an accruing strategy each field is decoded
      inside a `focus(CellRef(…))`, and the record is constructed only when every field
      decoded cleanly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvFormat
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class FullName(first: Text, last: Text)
      case class Person(id: Int, name: FullName, age: Int)

      t"1,Nick,Bottom,36".read[Sheet].rows(Prim).let(_.as[Person])
      ```
  entry .encodableDerivation   derives a row encoder for any product type, concatenating its fields' cells
      A nested case class contributes its own cells in place, so encoding is the mirror of
      `.decodableDerivation`'s positional reading.
  entry .showable   renders a row as one line of delimited text in the `Dsv.Format` in scope
      Cells are joined with the format's delimiter. A cell is quoted only if it contains the
      quote character, the delimiter, a line feed or a carriage return, and a quote within a
      quoted cell is doubled.
  entry .aggregableDerived   under `codecs.derived`, reads `Play in Dsv` through the row decoder even where `Play` has a `Dsv.Parsable`
      Selected by `import codecs.derived`, it outranks the direct path for a type which has a
      `Dsv.Parsable` in scope, so a program can compare the two, or keep the accruing row
      decoder for one read.
  entry .aggregableStrict   under `codecs.strict`, reads `Play in Dsv` directly, through the `Dsv.Parsable` in scope
      Paired with `Dsv1#aggregableStrictFailure`: under `import codecs.strict` a type with a
      `Dsv.Parsable` is parsed directly, and one without is a compile error naming the type,
      instead of falling back to the row decoder.
  entry .aggregableParsedList   `read[List[Play] in Dsv]`: parses every row directly, through `Play`'s `Dsv.Parsable`
      There is no row-decoder counterpart for a whole sheet, so this read is direct in every
      `codecs` mode, and needs a `Dsv.Parsable` for the element type (which `codecs.direct`
      derives for any product). Needs a `Dsv.Format`, a `Tactic[Dsv.Error]` and a
      `Buffering`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvFormat
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Stat(name: Text, count: Int)
      given Stat is Dsv.Parsable = Dsv.Parsable.derived

      t"a,1\nb,2".read[List[Stat] in Dsv]   // List(Stat(t"a", 1), Stat(t"b", 2))
      ```
  entry .Parsing   the common parent of `Dsv.Parsable` and `Dsv.Field`: reads a value off a `DsvReader`
    detail
        A `distillate.Parsable` whose `#Transport` is `Dsv` and whose `#Reader` is a
        `DsvReader`. An instance occupies `#width` cells of the current row and reads them,
        from a given offset, with `#parse`. Neither `Parsable` nor `Field` is a subtype of the
        other: `Parsable` is the opt-in surface which switches a read to the direct path, and
        `Field` the operational one which every field type resolves.
    refer caesura.DsvReader
    entry #Transport   the transport format: `Dsv`
    entry #Reader   the token reader instances parse from: `DsvReader`
    entry #width   the number of cells the value occupies in a positional row; `1` unless overridden
    entry #parse   reads the value from the current row of a `DsvReader`, from a cell offset (`0` by default)
  entry .Parsable2   the low-priority home of `Dsv.Parsable.generated`, the `codecs.direct` derivation
    hidden
    detail
        The parent of the `Dsv.Parsable` companion, holding the given which `codecs.direct`
        enables. Not for direct use.
    entry #generated   under `codecs.direct`, a `Dsv.Parsable` derived for every product type
        A type with a `Dsv.Parsable` of its own uses that instead. The derivation reads each
        field through `Dsv.Field`, so a product with a field which is not `Decodable in Text`
        (or a product of such fields) fails to derive rather than falling back.
  entry .Parsable   the opt-in typeclass for parsing a type straight from the source, with no `Dsv` per row
    detail
        A type which is `Dsv.Parsable` is read by `read[Play in Dsv]`, `read[List[Play] in Dsv]`
        and `rowsOf[Play]` directly from the parser's reusable row buffer, its cells addressed
        in place, so no `Dsv` row value is built for each record. Declaring one switches the
        path; a type without one keeps the row decoder, unless `codecs.direct` is imported.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import dsvFormats.csvFormat
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Stat(name: Text, count: Int, note: Optional[Text])

        object Stat:
          given parsable: Stat is Dsv.Parsable = Dsv.Parsable.derived

        t"z,9,note".read[Stat in Dsv]                                 // Stat(t"z", 9, t"note")
        t"x,1\ny,2".source[Text].rowsOf[Stat].map(_.count).to(List)   // List(1, 2)
        ```

        Fields are matched by heading under a header-bearing format, and positionally
        otherwise, and an `Optional` field the row does not reach is `Unset`. A direct parse
        fails fast: it does not accrue every bad cell as the row decoder does.

        The companion inherits the `codecs.direct` derivation, `generated`, from its parent
        `Dsv.Parsable2`.
    refer caesura.Dsv.Field
    refer caesura.DsvReader
    entry .derived   derives a `Dsv.Parsable` for a product type, each field read through its `Dsv.Field`
        `given Play is Dsv.Parsable = Dsv.Parsable.derived`, or `derives Dsv.Parsable`. Every
        field must have a `Dsv.Field`, which any `Decodable in Text` type, any `Optional` of
        one, and any product of such fields has.
    entry .fromField   makes a `Dsv.Parsable` from a `Dsv.Field`, keeping its width
  entry .Field   how one field of a directly-parsed product reads its cells from a `DsvReader`
    detail
        The typeclass the `Dsv.Parsable` derivation summons for each field. It always
        resolves for a type which is `Decodable in Text`, which reads one cell; a nested
        product is a `Field` of the summed width of its own fields (through
        `Dsv.ParsableDerivation`); and an `Optional` field is one too. A missing cell aborts
        with `Dsv.Error.Reason.Absent` through the reader's tactic, except where
        `.optional` intercepts it.
    entry .decodable   any type which is `Decodable in Text`, read from one cell
    entry .optional   an `Optional` field: `Unset` for a missing cell, as the `optionalityOptions` policies say
        As `Dsv.optionalDecodable` for the row decoder: a missing cell is `Unset` unless
        absence is strict, and a cell the inner field rejects raises unless faults are
        lenient, when it is `Unset`.
    entry .parseField   calls a field's `#parse` on a reader held as a neutral reference; for generated code
        Not for direct use: the derived parse lambda cannot take the reader capability as a
        parameter, so it carries it as an `AnyRef` and calls through this method.
    entry .fieldColumn   the column index of a heading on a reader held as a neutral reference; for generated code
        Not for direct use. `Unset` in positional mode.
  entry .ParsableDerivation   the product derivation of `Dsv.Field`, behind `Dsv.Parsable.derived`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: `Dsv.Parsable.derived` calls it. Under a header-bearing format a
        field is found by its column name; otherwise each field reads its span from a running
        offset.
    entry .DsvProductParser   the `Dsv.Field` a derivation produces: a width and a parse function
      entry #width   the total span of the product's fields
      entry #parse   reads the product from the current row, from a cell offset
    entry .conjunction   the macro which derives a `Dsv.Field` for a product
  entry .DecodableDerivation   the product derivation of `Decodable in Dsv`, behind `Dsv.decodableDerivation`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: the given `Dsv.decodableDerivation` calls it. It traverses the
        fields once, decoding each from its span (or, under a header, its named column), and
        under an accruing strategy focuses each on a `CellRef` and builds the record only when
        every field succeeded.
    entry .DsvProductDecoder   the `Decodable in Dsv` a derivation produces, wrapping its decode function
      entry #Self   the product type decoded
      entry #Form   the form decoded from: `Dsv`
      entry #decoded   decodes a row to the product
    entry .conjunction   the macro which derives a row decoder for a product
  entry .EncodableDerivation   the product derivation of `Encodable in Dsv`, behind `Dsv.encodableDerivation`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: the given `Dsv.encodableDerivation` calls it.
    entry .conjunction   the macro which derives a row encoder for a product, concatenating its fields' cells
  entry .Error   raised when delimited text cannot be parsed, or a row cannot be decoded
    detail
        Error 364, with a subcode from its `#reason`. A **parse** failure, a quote found after
        the start of an unquoted cell, reads _could not parse row data at row `row`, column
        `column` because a quote was found after the start of a cell_, and carries the 1-based
        row and column and the character offset into the input. A **decode** failure reads
        _could not decode the cell because the cell was absent_, or _because the value `value`
        could not be parsed as `expected`_; its position is not in the error itself (`#row` and
        `#column` are `Prim`, `#offset` is `0`) but in the `CellRef` focus an accruing
        strategy records with it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import dsvFormats.csvFormat

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

        safely(t"Nym,many".read[Person in Dsv])   // Unset
        ```

        Raised through the `Tactic[Dsv.Error]` in scope by `read[Sheet]`, `read[Play in Dsv]`,
        `rows`, `rowsOf` and `Dsv#as`. `Reason` is the enum `MisplacedQuote` (subcode 1, a
        parse failure), `Absent` (2, a cell missing from a short row or a header column the
        row lacks) and `Unparseable(value, expected)` (3, a cell its decoder rejected, with
        the cell's text and the name of the type expected).
    entry .communicable   renders a `Dsv.Error.Reason` as the clause the error's message ends with
    entry .Reason   why delimited data failed: `MisplacedQuote`, `Absent`, or `Unparseable(value, expected)`
      detail
          `MisplacedQuote` (subcode 1) is a parse failure: a quote character found after the
          start of an unquoted cell. `Absent` (2) is a decode failure for a cell the row does
          not have. `Unparseable(value, expected)` (3) is a decode failure for a cell whose
          text, `value`, could not be read as the type named by `expected`.
      entry #number   the reason's subcode, which follows the error number 364
    entry .apply   makes a decode-phase error from a format and a reason, with no position
        The position is filled with placeholders (`Prim`, `Prim`, `0`), since a decoder's
        position is carried by the `CellRef` it is focused on.
    entry #format   the `Dsv.Format` the data was read under
    entry #reason   the `Dsv.Error.Reason` for the failure
    entry #row   the 1-based row of a parse failure; `Prim` for a decode failure
    entry #column   the 1-based column of a parse failure; `Prim` for a decode failure
    entry #offset   the character (UTF-16 code-unit) offset of a parse failure into the decoded text; `0` for a decode failure
        A byte offset is not available: the parser reads text which has already been decoded
        from bytes.
  entry .Format   the conventions of a delimited format: header row, delimiter, quote and escape characters
    detail
        A `Dsv.Format` says whether the data's first row is a header (`#header`), which
        character separates cells (`#delimiter`), which quotes a cell (`#quote`), and which
        escapes a quote (`#escape`). Nothing parses or renders without one in scope; the
        standard ones are in `dsvFormats`, imported by name, and any other is a value bound
        as a local given:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        given Dsv.Format = Dsv.Format(header = false, delimiter = ';', quote = '"', escape = '"')

        t"a;\"b;c\"".read[Sheet].rows(Prim)   // Dsv(t"a", t"b;c")
        ```

        Under a header-bearing format, `read[Sheet]` takes the first row as the column names,
        so each row's cells can be addressed by name and a derived decoder matches fields to
        columns by name. Both the parser and the renderer escape a quote within a quoted cell
        by doubling it, which is the convention every standard format uses.
    refer caesura.dsvFormats
    entry #header   whether the first row names the columns
    entry #delimiter   the character between cells: `,`, a tab, or a space for the standard formats
    entry #quote   the character which encloses a cell containing the delimiter, a quote or a line break
    entry #escape   the character which escapes a quote within a quoted cell; the same as `#quote` for every standard format
    entry #Delimiter   the delimiter as a stable identifier, for use in patterns
    entry #Quote   the quote character as a stable identifier, for use in patterns
    entry #Escape   the escape character as a stable identifier, for use in patterns
    entry #doublingEscapes   whether a quote is escaped by doubling it: whether `#escape` is `#quote`
  entry .Redesignation   how a camel-case member name is mapped to a header name, for dynamic access to a row's cells
    detail
        A single-method trait: `#transform` maps a Scala name such as `targetPerson` to the
        header text it should match, such as `Target Person`. `Dsv#selectDynamic` needs one
        in scope, so `row.targetPerson[Text]` reads the column `Target Person`. The standard
        ones are in `dsvRedesignations`, imported by name; there is no default, and a lambda
        from `Text` to `Text` is one.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import dsvRedesignations.capitalizedWordsRedesignation

        summon[Dsv.Redesignation].transform(t"targetPerson")   // t"Target Person"
        ```

        A redesignation applies to dynamic member access only: a derived decoder matches each
        field to the column of exactly the field's name.
    refer caesura.dsvRedesignations
    entry #transform   maps a member name to the header text it should match
  entry #data   the row's cells, in order
  entry #columns   the index of each column by its name, where the data had a header; `Unset` otherwise
  entry #as   decodes the row as a type which is `Decodable in Dsv`, such as any case class
      Needs a `Tactic[Dsv.Error]`, and accrues through the `Foci[CellRef]` in scope, which is
      the default unless an accruing strategy such as `Validate[…, CellRef]` supplies one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvFormat
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class FullName(first: Text, last: Text)

      Dsv(t"Nick", t"Bottom").as[FullName]   // FullName(t"Nick", t"Bottom")
      ```
  entry #header   the column names in order, where the row has them; `Unset` otherwise
  entry #apply   reads the cell of a named column, decoded to any type `Decodable in Text`
      `row[Int](t"age")` is `Unset` if the row has no column names or no such column, and
      otherwise the cell decoded by the type's `Decodable in Text`, which may raise through
      its own tactic.
  entry #selectDynamic   reads the cell of a column as a member, `row.age[Int]`, with dynamic access enabled
      Needs `Dsv is Dynamical` — `import dynamicAccess.dynamicDsv`, or a `dynamically[Dsv]:`
      block — and a `Dsv.Redesignation`, which maps the member name to the column name. The
      result is `Optional`, `Unset` where the row has no such column.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvWithHeaderFormat
      import dsvRedesignations.unchangedRedesignation
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicDsv
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val row = t"greeting,number\nhello,23".read[Sheet].rows(Prim)
      row.let(_.number[Int])   // 23
      ```

entry .Dsv1   the middle-priority givens of the `Dsv` companion: the direct read of `Play in Dsv`, and the `codecs.strict` failure
  hidden
  detail
      A parent of the `Dsv` companion, so its givens are in `Dsv`'s implicit scope at a lower
      priority than the companion's own. Not referred to directly.
  entry #aggregableParsed   `read[Play in Dsv]` through `Play`'s `Dsv.Parsable`: the first row, parsed directly
      Outranks the row decoder in `Dsv2`, so declaring a `Dsv.Parsable` switches the path.
      An input with no data row raises `Dsv.Error.Reason.Absent`. Needs a `Dsv.Format`, a
      `Tactic[Dsv.Error]` and a `Buffering`.
  entry #aggregableStrictFailure   under `codecs.strict`, a compile error for a `Play in Dsv` read with no direct codec
      An inline given which names the type in the error, raised at the use site after
      selection, so that `codecs.strict` reports a type the direct path cannot read rather
      than letting the row decoder take it.

entry .Dsv2   the lowest-priority givens of the `Dsv` companion: the text fallback, single-record writes and the row-decoder read
  hidden
  detail
      A parent of the `Dsv` companion, below `Dsv1`, holding the instances any type takes
      only when nothing more specific applies. Not referred to directly.
  entry #decoder   any type `Decodable in Text`, decoded from the first cell of a row
      Held below the primitive cell decoders, which accrue errors, so `Int` and the rest use
      those; this one aborts on a bad cell through the text decoder.
  entry #encodableOver   `play.in[Text over Dsv]`: a value as one record of text in the `Dsv.Format` in scope
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvFormat

      case class FullName(first: Text, last: Text)

      FullName(t"Nick", t"Bottom").in[Text over Dsv]   // t"Nick,Bottom"
      ```
  entry #encodableOverData   `play.in[Data over Dsv]`: a value as one record, encoded as bytes in the `Codepage` in scope
  entry #aggregableIn   `read[Play in Dsv]` through the row decoder: the first row, decoded with `Play`'s `Decodable in Dsv`
      The path for a type with no `Dsv.Parsable`, and for any type under `codecs.derived`.
      Reading from a stream parses only as far as the first record extends. Needs a
      `Dsv.Format`, a `Tactic[Dsv.Error]` and a `Buffering`.

entry .Sheet   delimited data held in memory: every row, with the format and column names it was read under
  detail
      A `Sheet` is the materialized form of delimited data: its `#rows` are an array of `Dsv`
      rows, replayable, indexable and cheap to compare, with the `#format` they were parsed
      under and the `#columns` named by a header row, where there was one. Text reads as a
      sheet with `read[Sheet]` under a `Dsv.Format`, and a sheet renders back to text with
      `show` under one; a list of encodable values becomes a sheet with `dsv`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvWithHeaderFormat
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Measurement(name: Text, age: Int)

      val sheet: Sheet = t"name,age\nPercy,30\nHal,25".read[Sheet]
      sheet.columns                          // Array(t"name", t"age")
      sheet.as[Measurement]                  // List(Measurement(t"Percy", 30), Measurement(t"Hal", 25))
      sheet.rows(Prim).let(_[Text](t"name")) // t"Percy"
      ```

      The format decides how the same rows are written out, quoting a cell which holds its
      delimiter: in CSV the comma needs quoting, in TSV nothing does, and space-separated
      values quote the space:

      ```scala fuel
      alternative CSV
        code
            locally:
              import dsvFormats.csvFormat
              Sheet(Array(Dsv(t"Nick", t"Bottom, weaver"))).show   // t"Nick,\"Bottom, weaver\""
      alternative TSV
        code
            locally:
              import dsvFormats.tsvFormat
              Sheet(Array(Dsv(t"Nick", t"Bottom, weaver"))).show   // t"Nick\tBottom, weaver"
      alternative Space-separated
        code
            locally:
              import dsvFormats.ssvFormat
              Sheet(Array(Dsv(t"Nick", t"Bottom, weaver"))).show   // t"Nick \"Bottom, weaver\""
      ##
      ```

      A file too large to hold needs no sheet: `rows` on a character stream yields the rows
      one at a time through the same parser, and `rowsOf` parses each straight to a type.
      A sheet is also `Tabular`, so `escritoire` renders it as a table with a column per
      header (or per position), and it is the body of an HTTP response, sent as `text/csv`,
      or as `text/tab-separated-values` when its format's delimiter is a tab.
  refer caesura.Dsv
  refer caesura.Dsv.Format
  refer caesura.rows
  entry .aggregable   reads text as a `Sheet`, in the `Dsv.Format` in scope
      The given behind `read[Sheet]`. Quoted cells may contain the delimiter, line breaks and
      doubled quotes, and may span the chunks of a stream. Under a header-bearing format the
      first row is taken as the column names: it is not one of the `#rows`, each row's
      `Dsv#columns` maps the names to indices, and the sheet's `#columns` lists them. Needs a
      `Tactic[Dsv.Error]`, through which a quote found after the start of an unquoted cell is
      raised as `Dsv.Error.Reason.MisplacedQuote`.
  entry .showable   renders a sheet as delimited text in the `Dsv.Format` in scope, one line per row
      Rows are joined with a line feed, with no trailing one; each row is rendered as
      `Dsv.showable` renders it, quoting only the cells which need it. A header is not
      written: the sheet's `#columns` are not rendered.
  entry .streamable   renders a sheet as a stream of text, one row and a line feed per chunk
      Needs a `Dsv.Format`. This is what lets a sheet be written to any sink which takes a
      stream of `Text`.
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering of a sheet's format, column names and cells, needing no format
      `Sheet(format:○ ╱ columns:○ ╱ rows:⟦⟨ t"hello" t"world" ⟩⟧)`: each cell inspected as
      `Text`, so a cell which contains a delimiter cannot be mistaken for two, as it could in
      the `show` rendering.
  entry .tabular   renders a sheet as an `escritoire` table, with a column per header name or per position
      The columns are the sheet's `#columns` where it has them, and otherwise the numbers
      `1`, `2`, … up to the width of the first row; each column is collapsible, and a cell
      the row lacks is empty.
  entry .abstractable   sends a sheet as an HTTP body, as `text/csv`, or `text/tab-separated-values` for a tab delimiter
      Needs a `Codepage` to encode the text and a `Dsv.Format`; the media type is chosen from
      the delimiter of the sheet's own `#format`.
  entry #rows   the rows, in order, each a `Dsv`; the header row is not among them
  entry #format   the `Dsv.Format` the sheet was parsed under, or `Unset` for one built by hand
  entry #columns   the column names from the header row, in order; `Unset` if the data had no header
  entry #as   decodes every row as a type which is `Decodable in Dsv`, into a `List`
      Needs a `Tactic[Dsv.Error]` and a `Foci[CellRef]`, which is the default unless an
      accruing strategy supplies one.

entry .DsvReader   the token reader a `Dsv.Parsable` reads from: one row at a time, its cells addressed in place
  detail
      A `DsvReader` is the direct parser's view of the input: it advances one data row at a
      time with `#nextRow` and exposes the current row's cells, held in the parser's reusable
      buffer, by index with `#cell` and by heading with `#column`, so a `Dsv.Parsable` reads a
      record with no `Dsv` row value built. It carries the format and the error tactic, so
      instances need neither. It is an exclusive, stateful capability, owned by the read for
      its duration: nothing of it may be retained in the value parsed.

      Readers are made only by caesura itself, for `read[Play in Dsv]`, `read[List[Play] in
      Dsv]` and `rowsOf`; a program meets one only when writing a `Dsv.Parsable` or
      `Dsv.Field` by hand, rather than deriving it. The class is not exported to the
      `soundness` umbrella, and is named `caesura.DsvReader`.
  refer caesura.Dsv.Parsable
  refer caesura.Dsv.Field
  entry #absent   aborts the read with `Dsv.Error.Reason.Absent`, for a cell the row lacks
  entry #nextRow   advances to the next data row, consuming the header row first under a header format; `false` at the end
  entry #cells   the number of cells in the current row
  entry #cell   the cell at an index of the current row, or `Unset` if the row is shorter
  entry #column   the index of a heading, or `Unset` in positional mode or for an unknown heading

entry .CellRef   the position of a cell being decoded: the focus each accrued `Dsv.Error` is recorded with
  detail
      When a row decodes into a product under an accruing strategy, each field is decoded
      inside a `focus(CellRef(…))`, so each error the strategy collects comes with the
      `CellRef` of the column it was found in. `Validate[…, CellRef]` names it as the focus
      type, and its handler reads it as `prior`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvWithHeaderFormat
      import dysasymptotics.linearSize
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      case class Issues(items: List[(Text, Dsv.Error)] = Nil)(using Diagnostics)
      extends Error(m"${items.size} decoding issues"):
        def +(column: Text, error: Dsv.Error): Issues = Issues(items :+ (column, error))

      case class Measurement(name: Text, age: Int, height: Int)

      val damaged = t"name,age,height\nPercy,thirty,tall".read[Sheet].rows(Prim)

      Validate[Issues, [r] =>> r raises Dsv.Error, CellRef]
        ( Issues(),
          { case error: Dsv.Error => accrual + (prior.let(_.column).or(t"#"), error) } )
      . protect(damaged.let(_.as[Measurement]))
      . items.map(_(0))   // List(t"age", t"height")
      ```
  refer caesura.Dsv.Error
  entry #ordinal   the row of the cell; the derived decoder, which sees one row, always records `Prim`
  entry #column   the name of the field being decoded, which is the column's name under a header

entry .Spannable   how many cells a type occupies in a positional row, field by field
  detail
      A `Spannable` gives the spans of a type's fields: one cell for a field which is
      `Decodable in Text`, the span of the inner type for an `Optional` field, and the total
      of its own fields' spans for a nested case class. The row decoder and the direct parser
      use it to know where each field of a positional row begins, without discovering it
      while parsing. `Spannable.derived` gives the instance for any product type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class FullName(first: Text, last: Text)
      case class Person(id: Int, name: FullName, age: Int)

      Spannable.derived[Person].spans()   // Array(1, 2, 1): four cells in all
      ```

      Positional decoding is brittle by nature: a field added to a nested case class moves
      every column after it, with nothing in the file to say so. A header-bearing format,
      which matches fields to columns by name, avoids it.
  entry .conjunction   the macro which derives a product's spans, one per field
  entry .decoder   a span of one cell, for any type which is `Decodable in Text`
  entry .optional   an `Optional` field spans exactly as many cells as its inner type
      A present value occupies the inner span, and an absent one leaves those cells empty.
  entry #spans   the span of each field, in order; for a single-cell type, `Array(1)`

entry .dsv   encodes a value as a `Dsv` row, or a list of values as a `Sheet`
    An extension on any value which is `Encodable in Dsv`, which every product type derives,
    and on a `List` of them. The result renders as text with `show` under a `Dsv.Format`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import dsvFormats.csvFormat

    case class FullName(first: Text, last: Text)

    FullName(t"Nick", t"Bottom").dsv                                     // Dsv(t"Nick", t"Bottom")
    List(FullName(t"Nick", t"Bottom"), FullName(t"Francis", t"Flute")).dsv.show
    // t"Nick,Bottom\nFrancis,Flute"
    ```

entry .rows   the rows of a character stream, parsed one at a time, as a single-use `Iterator[Dsv]`
    The streaming counterpart of `read[Sheet]`, which holds every row: an extension on a
    `Stream[Text] over Credit`, which it consumes, yielding each row as it is parsed. A
    quoted cell may span chunk and line boundaries, since the parser carries its state
    across refills. Under a header-bearing format the header row is not yielded, but each
    row knows its column names. Needs a `Dsv.Format`, a `Tactic[Dsv.Error]` and a
    `Buffering`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import dsvFormats.csvWithHeaderFormat
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    t"name,age\nalpha,1\nbeta,2".source[Text].rows.map(_[Text](t"name").or(t"?")).to(List)
    // List(t"alpha", t"beta")
    ```

entry .rowsOf   the rows of a character stream, each parsed straight to a type through its `Dsv.Parsable`
    `stream.rowsOf[Play]` consumes the stream and yields one `Play` per data row, read directly
    from the parser's buffer with no `Dsv` built: the fastest way through a large file.
    `Play` needs a `Dsv.Parsable`, declared or (under `codecs.direct`) derived. Needs a
    `Dsv.Format`, a `Tactic[Dsv.Error]` and a `Buffering`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import dsvFormats.csvFormat
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Stat(name: Text, count: Int)
    given Stat is Dsv.Parsable = Dsv.Parsable.derived

    t"x,1\ny,2".source[Text].rowsOf[Stat].map(_.count).to(List)   // List(1, 2)
    ```

entry .dsvFormats   the standard delimited formats — CSV, TSV and space-separated, with and without a header — to import by name
  detail
      Every parse and every rendering needs a `Dsv.Format` in scope, and the choice is never
      implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is imported by name.
      Each quotes with `"` and escapes a quote by doubling it.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Positional
        code
            locally:
              import dsvFormats.csvFormat
              import strategies.throwUnsafely
              t"last,first\nBottom,Nick".read[Sheet].rows.length   // 2: the header is just another row
      alternative With a header
        code
            locally:
              import dsvFormats.csvWithHeaderFormat
              import strategies.throwUnsafely
              t"last,first\nBottom,Nick".read[Sheet].rows.length   // 1, with columns `last` and `first`
      ##
      ```

      A format with another delimiter is a `Dsv.Format` value bound as a local given.
  refer caesura.Dsv.Format
  entry .csvFormat   comma-separated values, with no header row
  entry .csvWithHeaderFormat   comma-separated values whose first row names the columns
  entry .tsvFormat   tab-separated values, with no header row
  entry .tsvWithHeaderFormat   tab-separated values whose first row names the columns
  entry .ssvFormat   space-separated values, with no header row
  entry .ssvWithHeaderFormat   space-separated values whose first row names the columns

entry .dsvRedesignations   the mappings from camel-case member names to header names, for dynamic access, to import by name
  detail
      `Dsv#selectDynamic`, the member form `row.targetPerson[Text]`, needs a
      `Dsv.Redesignation` in scope to find the column a member names, and there is no
      default: one is imported by name, or a lambda from `Text` to `Text` is bound as a local
      given.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Unchanged
        code
            locally:
              import dsvRedesignations.unchangedRedesignation
              summon[Dsv.Redesignation].transform(t"targetPerson")   // t"targetPerson"
      alternative Capitalized words
        code
            locally:
              import dsvRedesignations.capitalizedWordsRedesignation
              summon[Dsv.Redesignation].transform(t"targetPerson")   // t"Target Person"
      alternative Lower-case words
        code
            locally:
              import dsvRedesignations.lowerWordsRedesignation
              summon[Dsv.Redesignation].transform(t"targetPerson")   // t"target person"
      ##
      ```
  refer caesura.Dsv.Redesignation
  entry .unchangedRedesignation   the member name as it is: `targetPerson` matches the header `targetPerson`
  entry .capitalizedWordsRedesignation   capitalized words separated by spaces: `targetPerson` matches `Target Person`
  entry .lowerWordsRedesignation   lower-case words separated by spaces: `targetPerson` matches `target person`
  entry .lowerDottedRedesignation   lower-case words, meant for dotted headers such as `target.person`
      As implemented, the words are joined with spaces, so this gives `target person`, the
      same as `lowerWordsRedesignation`.
  entry .lowerSlashedRedesignation   lower-case words, meant for slashed headers such as `target/person`
      As implemented, the words are joined with spaces, so this gives `target person`, the
      same as `lowerWordsRedesignation`.

entry .dynamicAccess   the given which lets a row's cells be read as members, to import by name
  detail
      Dynamic member access is off unless `Dsv is Dynamical` is in scope, so a misspelt member
      is not silently a column lookup. `import dynamicAccess.dynamicDsv` grants it for the
      rest of the scope; `dynamically[Dsv]:` grants it for one block.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvWithHeaderFormat
      import dsvRedesignations.unchangedRedesignation
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicDsv
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"greeting,number\nhello,23".read[Sheet].rows(Prim).let(_.number[Int])   // 23
      ```
  entry .dynamicDsv   `Dsv is Dynamical`: enables `row.column[T]` for every row in scope
      An erased witness; it costs nothing at runtime. The `optics.dsvCellLens` given needs it
      too.

entry .optionalityOptions   the policies for an `Optional` field's missing or malformed cell, to import by name
  detail
      An `Optional` field of a decoded row meets two situations: its cell is **absent** (a
      short positional row, or a header column the row lacks), or its cell is present but its
      inner decoder **rejects** it. By default an absent cell is `Unset` and a rejected one an
      error; each is changed by importing one of these givens by name. An empty cell is a
      present value, not an absent one.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Default
        code
            locally:
              import dsvFormats.csvFormat
              case class Person(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])
              safely(t"Nym,many".read[Person in Dsv])   // Unset: the whole read failed
      alternative Lenient faults
        code
            locally:
              import dsvFormats.csvFormat
              import optionalityOptions.lenientDsvFaults
              case class Person(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])
              safely(t"Nym,many".read[Person in Dsv])   // Person(t"Nym", Unset)
      ##
      ```

      They govern both the row decoder (`Dsv.optionalDecodable`) and the direct parser
      (`Dsv.Field.optional`).
  entry .strictDsvAbsence   an absent cell for an `Optional` field raises `Dsv.Error.Reason.Absent`
  entry .lenientDsvAbsence   an absent cell for an `Optional` field is `Unset`; the default
  entry .strictDsvFaults   a cell the inner decoder rejects raises `Dsv.Error`, even for an `Optional` field; the default
  entry .lenientDsvFaults   a cell the inner decoder rejects is `Unset` for an `Optional` field
      The inner decode runs under `tolerate`, so its error is discarded rather than raised.

entry .optics   lenses over a row's cells by column name and over a sheet's rows, to import by name
  detail
      The `panopticon` optics for tabular data, which mirror the row-and-cell structure: a
      cell of a header-bearing row is addressed by its column name, and a sheet's rows by
      position, all at once, or by a predicate. With them imported, `lens` updates a sheet in
      place of a hand-written copy.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dsvFormats.csvWithHeaderFormat
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicDsv
      import optics.{dsvCellLens, dsvRowEachOptical, dsvRowFilterOptical, dsvRowOptical}
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val sheet: Sheet = t"name,age\nPercy,30\nHal,25".read[Sheet]

      sheet.lens(_(Sec).name = t"Poins")                                // Hal becomes Poins
      sheet.lens(_(Each).name = t"X")                                   // every name becomes X
      sheet.lens(_(Filter[Dsv](_.data.head == t"Hal")).name = t"X")     // only Hal's
      ```
  entry .dsvCellLens   a lens from a row onto the cell of a named column, `_.name`
      Needs `Dsv is Dynamical` (`dynamicAccess.dynamicDsv`). Reading a column the row lacks
      gives the empty text, and writing one leaves the row unchanged. The column name is the
      member name as written: no `Dsv.Redesignation` is applied.
  entry .dsvRowOptical   addresses the row of a sheet at an `Ordinal`: `_(Sec)` is the second row
  entry .dsvRowEachOptical   addresses every row of a sheet: `_(Each)`
  entry .dsvRowFilterOptical   addresses the rows of a sheet matching a predicate: `_(Filter[Dsv](…))`

entry .construables   the media types `text/csv` and `text/tab-separated-values` construed as a `Sheet`, to import by name
  detail
      In the `caesura.http` component. A `Construable` tells an HTTP client which type to read
      a response body of a given media type as; nothing is construed without a deliberate
      choice, so each mapping is imported by name. With one in scope, an `apoplexy` client
      reads a CSV response as a `Sheet`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import construables.{csvConstruable, tsvConstruable}
      ```
  entry .csvConstruable   a `text/csv` body is a `Sheet`
  entry .tsvConstruable   a `text/tab-separated-values` body is a `Sheet`
